      "text": "To determine the type of curve on which all the points \\((x, y) = \\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\) lie, we will express \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in terms of \\(t\\) and then eliminate \\(t\\) to find a relation between \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[x = \\frac{t + 1}{t}\\]\n\\[y = \\frac{t - 1}{t}\\]\n\nFirst, we solve for \\(t\\) in terms of \\(x\\):\n\\[x = \\frac{t + 1}{t}\\]\n\\[x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t}\\]\n\\[x - 1 = \\frac{1}{t}\\]\n\\[t = \\frac{1}{x - 1}\\]\n\nNext, we solve for \\(t\\) in terms of \\(y\\):\n\\[y = \\frac{t - 1}{t}\\]\n\\[y = 1 - \\frac{1}{t}\\]\n\\[y - 1 = \\frac{1}{t}\\]\n\\[t = \\frac{1}{y - 1}\\]\n\nSince both expressions are equal to \\(t\\), we set them equal to each other:\n\\[x = \\frac{1}{y - 1}\\]\n\\[y - 1 = \\frac{1}{x}\\]\n\\[y = \\frac{1}{x} + 1\\]\n\\[y = \\frac{1 + x}{x}\\]\n\nThis is the equation of a hyperbola. Therefore, the points \\((x, y) = \\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\) all lie on a hyperbola.\n\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
