      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the type of curve on which the points \\(\\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\) lie for real numbers \\(t \\neq 0\\), we start by expressing the coordinates \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in terms of \\(t\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[ x = \\frac{t + 1}{t} \\]\n\\[ y = \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\]\n\nWe can simplify these expressions:\n\\[ x = \\frac{t + 1}{t} = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\\[ y = \\frac{t - 1}{t} = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\nNext, we eliminate the fraction by expressing \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in terms of a common variable. Let:\n\\[ x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\\[ y = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\nWe can solve for \\(t\\) in terms of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\). First, add the two equations:\n\\[ x + y = \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{t}\\right) + \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{t}\\right) = 2 \\]\n\\[ x + y = 2 \\]\n\nNext, subtract the second equation from the first:\n\\[ x - y = \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{t}\\right) - \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{t}\\right) = \\frac{2}{t} \\]\n\\[ x - y = \\frac{2}{t} \\]\n\nFrom \\(x + y = 2\\), solve for \\(\\frac{1}{t}\\):\n\\[ \\frac{1}{t} = 2 - x \\]\n\nFrom \\(x - y = \\frac{2}{t}\\), solve for \\(\\frac{1}{t}\\):\n\\[ \\frac{1}{t} = x - y \\]\n\nSince both expressions are equal to \\(\\frac{1}{t}\\), we can set them equal to each other:\n\\[ 2 - x = x - y \\]\n\\[ 2 - x = x - y \\]\n\\[ 2 = 2x - y \\]\n\\[ y = 2x - 2 \\]\n\nThus, the relationship between \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) is:\n\\[ y = 2x - 2 \\]\n\nThis is the equation of a straight line in the form \\(y = mx + b\\), where \\(m = 2\\) and \\(b = -2\\).\n\nTherefore, the points \\(\\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\) lie on a straight line. The correct option is \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
