      "text": "To determine the type of curve on which the points \\((x, y) = \\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\) lie, we start by expressing \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in terms of \\(t\\):\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{t + 1}{t} = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\\[ y = \\frac{t - 1}{t} = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\nNext, we can eliminate the parameter \\(t\\) by expressing \\(t\\) from one equation and substituting it into the other. From the equation for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[ x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\\[ t = \\frac{1 + x}{x} = \\frac{1}{x} + 1 \\]\n\nNow substitute \\(t = \\frac{1}{x} + 1\\) into the equation for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[ y = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} = 1 - \\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{x} + 1} = 1 - \\frac{x}{1 + x} = \\frac{1 + x - x}{1 + x} = \\frac{1}{1 + x} \\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\n\\[ y = \\frac{1}{1 + x} \\]\n\nThis equation represents a hyperbola. To see this more clearly, let's rewrite it in a standard form. From the equation \\(y = \\frac{1}{1 + x}\\), we can express \\(x\\) in terms of \\(y\\):\n\n\\[ y(1 + x) = 1 \\]\n\\[ y + yx = 1 \\]\n\\[ yx = 1 - y \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{1 - y}{y} = \\frac{1}{y} - 1 \\]\n\nThis is the equation of a hyperbola in the form \\(\\frac{x}{a} - \\frac{y}{b} = 1\\), where \\(a = -1\\) and \\(b = 1\\). Therefore, the points \\((x, y)\\) lie on a hyperbola.\n\nThe correct option is \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
