      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the type of curve on which all the points \\((x, y) = \\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\) lie, we need to express \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in terms of each other and then eliminate the parameter \\(t\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{t + 1}{t} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = \\frac{t - 1}{t}\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's solve for \\(t\\) in terms of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).\n\nFrom \\(x = \\frac{t + 1}{t}\\), we can rearrange to get:\n\\[\nx = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\implies x - 1 = \\frac{1}{t} \\implies t = \\frac{1}{x - 1}\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute \\(t = \\frac{1}{x - 1}\\) into the equation for \\(y\\):\n\\[\ny = \\frac{\\frac{1}{x - 1} - 1}{\\frac{1}{x - 1}} = \\frac{\\frac{1 - (x - 1)}{x - 1}}{\\frac{1}{x - 1}} = \\frac{\\frac{2 - x}{x - 1}}{\\frac{1}{x - 1}} = 2 - x\n\\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\\[\ny = 2 - x\n\\]\n\nThis is the equation of a line. Therefore, the points \\((x, y)\\) lie on a line.\n\nThe correct option is \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
